[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports hold three
problems. The queued fix is for the parser, which is one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 was closed on 05 Oct. None of its three problems is done yet: each ticket below says what is
still missing.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} {status} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads dates month first; every tenant writes the day first.
  A date with a day of 12 or less comes out swapped ("our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July").
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field but reads the date
  day first. People type what the hint says. Only reports sent through that form, from tenants
  on booking-v3.
- **`shift`**: the date moves by one day ("Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got
  Monday."). A swap cannot do that; the Lisbon/Toronto report points at a time zone. These are
  the reports on the desk's own list of one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`).

## Where each problem stands

Engineering's update on #4471 (05 Oct) is a status line, with no link, test or data behind it.

- **`parser`**: engineering says fixed ("fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales"). Asked on the
  ticket for the commit, the locales it covers (en-GB is not an EU locale, and a tenant here
  writes en-GB), a test, and reports after the fix. Bookings already made keep the swapped date.
- **`hint`**: won't fix, because the booking-v3 form is retired. That stops new cases if the form
  is off; bookings already made through it keep the swapped date, some of them still ahead. The
  tenant files here still list the three tenants on booking-v3.
- **`shift`**: not in engineering's update. Open.

The tickets stay open until each of these is answered. The export ends on 04 Oct, so nothing here
shows whether reports stopped after the fix.

## How the reports were told apart

- By tenant and channel (`causeOf`), never by the intake tag: the tag is a guess from the
  reporter's browser, and it disagrees with the tenant.
- One report per id: the export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The counts above are built from the export at render time. When the desk adds reports, the
  table follows.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subjects match,
without reading the body. The problems share their subjects ("Wrong day on the invite" comes in
all three), so three bugs became one ticket. Reading the body, or the tenant and channel, keeps
them apart.

